  "2. Opponent Strategy & Argument Evaluation": {
    "Their Two Primary Defensive Shields": [
      {
        "Shield 1: Jarvan IV Parity": "Honest members (especially 1, 4, 5, 8) argue that Jarvan IV EQ-Flash does not cancel animations or have an engine-level queue buffer; it is merely an active moving knockup aura displaced mid-drag. Thus, if Jarvan qualifies, Tryndamere cannot be excluded on the grounds of being an active moving hitbox translation.",
        "Our Counter": "Jarvan's EQ delivers discrete hard crowd control ('crow control from that dash ability' explicitly noted in the prompt) that creates an instantaneous collision interrupt/knockup upon contacting enemy hurtboxes. Tryndamere has zero crowd control and zero collision interaction—his model simply passes through units along a fixed 660-unit vector while a periodic aura ticks. Jarvan's aura forces a discrete state change on the target (airborne collision hit-confirm), fulfilling 'landing the skill'."
      },
      {
        "Shield 2: Disjunctive 'OR' in Prompt Preamble": "Honest members (especially 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9) argue that the prompt says 'cancel the animation, surprise opponents (lower chance for opponents to doge), OR ensure that the ability lands more effectively (buffer)', meaning Tryndamere satisfies the surprise/dodge condition even if he doesn't buffer/land.",
        "Our Counter": "Test construction and prompt syntax hierarchy: The preamble describes general community uses with 'or', but the trailing 'Note that...' paragraph is an explicit, mandatory restrictive constraint designed specifically to disqualify abilities: '(not for repositioning only or for other follow up abilities)'. In standard puzzle/test design, a closing restrictive 'Note that' overrides/narrows preamble generalities. If the 'or' in the intro governed, then ANY dash where Flash surprises someone would qualify, completely rendering the negative constraint null and void."
      }
    ],
    "Unanswered Vulnerability in Honest Bloc": "Colleagues 2, 5, and 9 completely ignored my Round 4 challenge regarding Renekton (E), Corki (W), and Kayn (Q). These abilities all deal native dash damage, yet are disqualified because Flashing mid-dash is repositioning an active damage box. Their claim that 'deals damage = inherently not repositioning only' completely collapses when pressed on Corki W and Renekton E."
  },
